## Unit Testing in Go

Go has a **built-in** testing framework — no external libraries needed. Test files have a `_test.go` suffix and test functions start with `Test`.

## Basic Test Structure

File: `mathutil_test.go`

```go
package mathutil

import "testing"

func TestAdd(t *testing.T) {
	result := Add(2, 3)
	if result != 5 {
		t.Errorf("Add(2, 3) = %d; want 5", result)
	}
}

func TestSubtract(t *testing.T) {
	got := Subtract(10, 4)
	want := 6
	if got != want {
		t.Errorf("Subtract(10, 4) = %d; want %d", got, want)
	}
}
```

## Running Tests

```bash
go test ./...          # all packages
go test -v ./...       # verbose output
go test -run TestAdd   # specific function
go test -count=1 ./... # disable test caching
```

## Methods of t *testing.T

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `t.Error(msg)` | Log error, continue test |
| `t.Errorf(format, args)` | Log formatted error |
| `t.Fatal(msg)` | Log error, stop test |
| `t.Fatalf(format, args)` | Log formatted error, stop |
| `t.Log(msg)` | Log message (only shown with `-v` or on failure) |
| `t.Skip(msg)` | Skip this test |

## Complete Example

File: `calculator.go`

```go
package calculator

import "errors"

func Divide(a, b float64) (float64, error) {
	if b == 0 {
		return 0, errors.New("division by zero")
	}
	return a / b, nil
}
```

File: `calculator_test.go`

```go
